<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edict of Rothari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">643</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Edict of Rothari was the first written law code of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, issued by King Rothari at Pavia in November 643. Written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, its 388 chapters set out Lombard customary law, including fixed money compensation for injuries in place of blood feud. Later kings such as Liutprand added to it, and it remained in use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for centuries.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to the 8th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-deacon">Paul the Deacon</a>, the customary law of the Lombards, the cawarfidae, had until then been kept in memory. King Rothari had it written down by a scribe of Lombard origin named Ansoald and promulgated it at Pavia on 22 November 643 before a gairethinx, an assembly of the army, which approved the law in the old Germanic way by clashing spears on shields. In the preamble Rothari set out his own lineage back eleven generations. The code came late: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a> had compiled theirs long before.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Although written in Vulgar Latin, the edict was mostly Germanic tribal law on weregilds, inheritance and duels rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>, and unlike the near-contemporary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigothic-code">Forum Iudicum</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> it shows no influence of canon law; its only church matter is a ban on violence in churches. Some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> words were left untranslated, among them gastald, mundius, aldius and launegild. Its changes to custom limited the power of the great lords and strengthened the king, and it gave military authority to the dukes and civil authority to a schulthais in the countryside and a gastald in the cities.</p><p>The edict says nothing of public life or trade and is minutely concerned with compensation for wrongs and the defence of property, with a price for each tooth, finger or toe. Free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> women were always under the wardship of their male kin, yet to injure a free woman or girl cost 900 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidi</a>, against 20 solidi for a free man without bodily harm. Injuries to a semi-free aldius or a household slave were priced above those to agricultural slaves. A father could disinherit his son only for certain grave crimes, and illegitimate children also had rights of inheritance. Trials used compurgation and the wager of battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The edict applied only to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>: the Roman population under Lombard rule expected to live by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>, while foreigners who settled in Lombard lands were to follow Lombard law unless the king allowed them another. By the reign of King Liutprand (712-743) most inhabitants of Lombard Italy were counted as Lombards, whatever their ancestry, and lived under this law.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edictum Rothari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rothari issued a written law code, the <b>Edict of Rothari</b>, in 643.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Kingdom of the Lombards</a></li></ul></section>
